<p>
  PhonePad makes an Android phone act as an Xbox controller for a Windows PC, over
  Wi-Fi. Measured on the development hardware, a thumb movement on the phone
  reaches the PC and is acknowledged back in 4.9 to 8.0 milliseconds, typically
  about five, at a steady 249 packets a second with no loss across 42,805
  consecutive packets. This article is about how that path is built: what runs
  where, why each piece was chosen over its alternatives, and the measurements
  that changed the design along the way.
</p>
<p>
  If you want to know what PhonePad is before how it works,
  <a href="/articles/introducing-phonepad/">Introducing PhonePad</a> is the
  better place to start.
</p>

<h2>The path</h2>
<p>
  Every input travels the same short route. On the phone, raw touch events go to
  a router that works out which control each finger is on, the router updates a
  plain controller state, and the state is sent as one UDP datagram. On the PC,
  the companion receives it, verifies it and writes it to a virtual Xbox 360
  controller, where games read it through XInput like any other pad.
</p>

<Figure caption="The hot path. Nothing in it waits on a user interface: on the phone, touch never rebuilds a widget; on the PC, the window only reads shared state and could freeze without stopping the controller." wide>
  <Pipeline />
</Figure>

<h2>How Windows sees a controller</h2>
<p>
  The first decision was the one everything else depends on: how a program
  creates a controller that Windows, and every game, will accept as real. There
  were four candidates.
</p>
<div class="table-scroll">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Option</th><th>Why it was or was not used</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Virtual HID Framework</td>
        <td>Microsoft's documented route, but it means writing a kernel driver and either an EV-signed submission or putting the PC into test-signing mode. Not something to ask of someone who wants to play a game today.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>GameInput</td>
        <td>An API for reading devices. It offers no supported way to publish a virtual one.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>vJoy</td>
        <td>Presents a DirectInput-only device. Xbox Cloud Gaming and most modern games read XInput or the browser's Gamepad API, so it would be invisible to them.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>ViGEmBus</strong></td>
        <td>Chosen. It creates a real Xbox 360 device node without driver signing, visible at once to <code>joy.cpl</code>, XInput, DirectInput, GameInput and the Gamepad API.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>
<p>
  ViGEmBus has a real weakness: its author archived it in November 2023 and its
  last release is final. That risk is written down rather than hidden, and the
  design contains it. The rest of the companion talks to a
  <code>VirtualPad</code> interface, so moving to a successor driver touches one
  file. It is also the mechanism Parsec and DS4Windows ship with, which says
  something about how dependable it has been in practice.
</p>
<p>
  The choice was verified rather than assumed. A small probe program plugs in a
  virtual pad, writes known states to it, and reads them back through Windows'
  own XInput API. All 26 checks passed (15 buttons, 5 trigger levels and 6 stick
  positions) and a write became visible to XInput in 0.09 milliseconds on
  average. The virtual pad costs essentially nothing. One oddity surfaced along
  the way: the public <code>XInputGetState</code> call hides the Guide button,
  and only an undocumented sibling reports it, so the probe looks that function
  up at run time in order to test Guide honestly.
</p>

<h2>Forty bytes over UDP</h2>
<p>
  Input is sent as fixed-layout binary packets: no JSON, no variable-length
  fields, every integer little-endian. A full controller state fits in 40 bytes,
  so even at the 250-a-second ceiling the stream is about ten kilobytes a second.
</p>

<Figure caption="The INPUT packet. The button field is bit-for-bit XInput's own wButtons, so the PC copies it straight through without translating. The last eight bytes authenticate the first 32." wide>
  <Packet />
</Figure>

<p>
  The transport is UDP, not TCP, and that is a deliberate choice about which
  failure is worse. TCP guarantees delivery by resending, and holds back
  everything after a lost packet until the resend arrives. For a controller, a
  resent input is a <em>stale</em> input: by the time it lands, the thumb has
  moved on. So PhonePad never resends. It sends the full state whenever it
  changes, and repeats it at 120 Hz even when it has not. A lost packet is
  corrected by the next one, within about eight milliseconds, and nothing
  waits for anything.
</p>

<h2>Security on a shared network</h2>
<p>
  An unauthenticated socket that injects controller input is a genuine hazard on
  a shared network, so no input is accepted from anything that has not paired.
  Pairing is an X25519 key exchange, confirmed by the six-digit code the companion
  displays, and it leaves both sides holding a shared key. Every input packet then
  carries the first eight bytes of an HMAC-SHA256 over its contents, and a sequence
  number checked against a 64-entry sliding window, so packets can be neither
  forged nor replayed. Input and control messages have separate sequence
  counters, so neither stream can disturb the other's replay protection.
</p>
<p>
  Rejected packets are counted by reason and shown in the companion's
  diagnostics. They are never dropped silently, because a silent drop is
  indistinguishable from a network problem when someone is trying to work out
  why their controller is not responding.
</p>

<h2>The phone side</h2>
<p>
  The app's interface is written in Flutter, and the obvious design would have
  moved the sending into native Android code. It was considered and rejected:
  routing every packet across the Flutter-to-Kotlin bridge adds a hop and an
  allocation to the hot path rather than removing one. Instead, raw pointer
  events feed a controller engine written in plain Dart, and the engine sends
  the datagram itself, from the same isolate, with one system call. The controller
  surface is drawn by a single custom painter behind a repaint boundary, so a
  touch never causes a widget rebuild or a layout pass.
</p>
<p>
  Kotlin is used only for what Dart cannot reach: asking for the display's
  120 Hz mode, holding Android's low-latency Wi-Fi lock, excluding the screen
  edges from the system's back gesture so it cannot fire mid-game, the vibration
  motor, reading the Wi-Fi link to compute the subnet's broadcast address for
  discovery, and a foreground service so the system cannot suspend an active
  session.
</p>
<p>
  One rule in the touch code is worth singling out. The router never toggles a
  button on or off in response to a single event. On every event it rebuilds the
  whole controller state from the set of fingers currently on the glass. That
  makes a stuck button structurally impossible rather than merely unlikely.
</p>

<h2>Failing safe</h2>
<p>
  The worst thing a controller can do is keep a button held after the
  connection dies: a car accelerating into a wall, a character running off a
  ledge. The companion therefore runs a watchdog. If no valid packet arrives for
  120 milliseconds, every control is released to neutral; after two seconds, the
  session is dropped and the phone must reconnect. When the companion exits, the
  virtual pad is unplugged.
</p>
<p>
  This was tested the hard way. A button was held on the phone and the app was
  force-stopped mid-press. The companion logged the stall at 131 milliseconds,
  and Windows saw exactly three states: neutral, the button, neutral. The
  11-millisecond overshoot is one pass of the receive loop, which is expected
  and bounded.
</p>

<h2>What measuring changed</h2>
<p>
  Several of PhonePad's design decisions came from measurements that
  contradicted an assumption. Three are worth retelling.
</p>

<h3>65 Hz instead of 250</h3>
<p>
  The first test sender, asked for 250 packets a second, managed 65. The
  protocol was not at fault. Windows rounds socket read timeouts up to its
  scheduler tick of about 15.6 milliseconds, so a "1 millisecond" timeout
  actually cost 15, and capped the loop. Sleeping is quantised the same way. The
  sender was rebuilt around a non-blocking socket and produced an exact 250 Hz.
  The lesson generalises: on Windows, any pacing built on sleeping is coarse.
</p>

<h3>A rate that read too high</h3>
<p>
  At one point the phone's own panel reported sending 312 packets a second while
  the PC reported accepting 259. It looked like loss. It was a measurement bug:
  each side published its count when "more than a second" had passed, but only
  checked on a periodic tick, so the real window was up to 25% longer than the
  second it was divided by. Both sides now divide by the time that actually
  elapsed, and both read 249. The documentation that had quoted the inflated
  figure was corrected in place, with the explanation left next to it.
</p>

<h3>Two deadzones</h3>
<p>
  The most noticeable improvement came from a complaint about feel: nothing at
  the start of the stick's travel, then suddenly too much. The cause was two
  deadzones stacked on each other. Games apply their own, typically 15 to 25% of
  the range, because they assume a physical stick with slop and drift to hide.
  PhonePad was applying 12% of its own before the game ever saw the value.
</p>

