<p>
  A thumb on glass does not glide; it sticks and then breaks free in a jump of two
  or three millimetres, around 12% of the stick's travel. Arriving just as the
  response finally began, that jump delivered about 16% of the game's input at
  once, which is exactly "nothing, then too much". The fix inverts the old
  behaviour: as soon as the thumb leaves the centre, PhonePad's output starts
  just past the game's own dead band, so what the player feels starts from zero
  and climbs smoothly. One subtlety had to be protected with a test: an exactly
  centred stick must still send exactly zero, or every game would read the pad
  as permanently pushed.
</p>

<h2>What has not been measured</h2>
<p>
  The project keeps a list of what it does not know yet, instead of filling the
  gaps with estimates: battery drain over a long session, CPU use on either side,
  the phone's frame rate under heavy touch, behaviour on a congested or weak
  network, and the noise from a real finger held still. The round-trip figure is
  also explicit about its scope. It covers phone to PC, validation, the virtual
  pad and back, but not the touchscreen's own sampling delay or the display at
  the other end, which the software cannot observe.
</p>

<h2>Building on it</h2>
<p>
  The wire protocol lives in its own folder of the repository, licensed under
  Apache-2.0 rather than the GPL that covers the app and companion, with a shared
  fixture file that both implementations are tested against. That is an
  invitation: a compatible client for another platform, or a companion for
  another operating system, can be written against the documented format without
  inheriting the GPL. The full reasoning behind every choice above is in the
  architecture decision record, and the raw measurements, with the commands to
  reproduce them, are in the benchmarks document.
</p>
